    "discipline": "Electrical and Computer",
    "subject": "Computer Systems",
    "chapter": "Interfacing",
    "topic": "Interfacing",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<ul>\r\n<li>An I/O interface consists of the circuitry required to connect an input/output device to a computer system.</li>\r\n<li>On one side of the interface have bus signals for address, data and control.</li>\r\n<li>On the other hand data path with its associated controls to transfer data between the interface and the input/output device.</li>\r\n<li>This side is called port. It can be classified as serial or parallel port.</li>\r\n<li><b>Parallel port</b> transfers data simultaneously to or from the device. It uses multiple pin connector and the circuit is simple.</li>\r\n<li><b>Serial port</b> transmits and receives data one bit at a time. For long distance, it is convenient and cost effective.</li>\r\n</ul>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
