    "discipline": "Electrical and Computer",
    "subject": "Electricity and Magnetism",
    "chapter": "Transmission and Distribution",
    "topic": "Real and Reactive Losses",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "Operation of Transmission Lines \r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Inductive reactance creates a reactive power demand (and a loss of reactive power) in the line that results in a drop in voltage at the receiving end </li>\r\n<li>As line becomes more reactive, current must increase for a given amount of Real Power </li>\r\n<li>Increase in current further increases reactive losses (recall that reactance >> resistance)</li> \r\n<li>Increased reactive losses results in larger voltage drop at receiving end </li>\r\n<li>Relatively higher inductive reactance implies that it is inefficient to deliver reactive power over long distances; it is better to compensate for reactive demand locally – Reactive power compensation devices include static devices (capacitors, inductors, etc) and dynamic (generators, synchronous condensers, etc)</li>\r\n</ul>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": []
