    "discipline": "Electrical and Computer",
    "subject": "Engineering Economics",
    "chapter": "Risk Identification",
    "topic": "Risk Terminology",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<ul>\r\n<li><b>Risk</b> is a situation of <b>perfect knowledge</b> where possible <b>outcomes</b> are known for a given <b>scenario</b> and the <b>probabilities</b> associated with each possible outcome is also known. As opposed to this, when <b>imperfect knowledge</b> exists, either in terms of lack of knowledge of possiblities or their probabilities, it is referred as <b>uncertainty</b>.</li>\r\n<li><b>Risk appetite</b>: The amount and type of risk that in investor (or an engineer) is prepared to seek, accept and tolerate.</li>\r\n<li><b>Risk management</b>: The forecasting and evaluation of financial risks together with the identification of procedures to avoid or minimize their impact.</li>\r\n<li><b>Risk Mitigation</b>: The process a business undertakes to reduce its exposure to the various risks it might face.</li>\r\n<li><b>Risk Impact or Severity</b>: The expected harm or adverse effect that may occur due to exposure to the Risk.</li>\r\n<ul>",
