    "discipline": "Electrical and Computer",
    "subject": "Ethics and Professional Practice",
    "chapter": "Public Safety, Health, Welfare",
    "topic": "Radiation Protection",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n    <div class=\"col-8\">\r\n   <p>Radiation protection involves measures and strategies to minimize exposure to ionizing radiation, electromagnetic fields, and other potentially harmful forms of radiation.</p>\r\n<p>Materials with high atomic numbers, such as lead and concrete, are commonly used for radiation shielding to absorb and reduce ionizing radiation.</p>\r\n<p>The Gray (Gy) is the SI unit for absorbed dose of ionizing radiation, while the Sievert (Sv) quantifies the biological effects of ionizing radiation, taking into account different types of radiation and their impact on human tissues.</p>\r\n<p>The ALARA (As Low As Reasonably Achievable) principle is fundamental in radiation protection, emphasizing that radiation exposure should be minimized to the lowest possible level, taking into account economic and societal factors.</p>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n <center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Strahlenschutz_uebung.jpg\" style=\"width:500px;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strahlenschutz_uebung.jpg\">Mkupi</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0/de/deed.en\">CC BY-SA 2.0 DE</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
