    "discipline": "Electrical and Computer",
    "subject": "Mathematics",
    "chapter": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Terminology and Symbols",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<h4><span class=\"badge bg-secondary\">Number Sets:</span></h4>\r\n\r\nN = {1, 2, . . .}, the set of Natural numbers;<br/>\r\nW = {0,1,2,...}, the set of whole numbers<br/>\r\nZ = {0,1,−1,2,−2,...}, the set of Integers;<br/>\r\nQ =${\\dfrac{p}{q}}$ :p,q∈Z, q $\\neq$ 0,the set of Rational numbers; <br/>\r\nR = the set of Real numbers; and<br/>\r\nC = the set of Complex numbers.<br/>\r\n\r\nWe will use $\\phi$ to denote an empty set.<br/>\r\n\r\n<h4><span class=\"badge bg-secondary\">Operations on Sets:</span></h4>\r\n\r\nUnion: Denoted by $\\cup$ symbol, such as $A \\cup B$.<br/>\r\nIntersection: Denoted by $\\cap$ symbol, such as $A \\cap B$\r\n\r\n<h4><span class=\"badge bg-secondary\">Symbols Used in Set Theory:</span></h4>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>To list the elements in a set, we use { } brackets.</li>\r\n<li>Subset : $\\subseteq$</li>\r\n<li>Not subset: $\\nsubseteq$</li>\r\n<li>Proper subset: $\\subset$</li>\r\n<li>Not Proper subset: $\\not\\subset$</li>\r\n</ul>",
