    "discipline": "Electrical and Computer",
    "subject": "Software Engineering",
    "chapter": "Data Structures",
    "topic": "Lists",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-6\">\r\n\r\n<p>A <b>linked list</b> is a linear collection of data elements. It has two parts: One is info and other is link part. Info part gives information and link part is address of next node.</p>\r\n\r\n<b>Advantages of Linked list:</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Linked lists are dynamic. Their size can be altered during runtime.</li>\r\n<li>Memory-efficient.</li>\r\n<li>Insertion and Deletion is easier.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<b>Advantages of Linked list:</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Consumes more space as additional space is required to store pointer address.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<b>Basic Operations</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Creation</li>\r\n<li>Insertion</li>\r\n<li>Deletion</li>\r\n<li>Traversing: It is the process of accessing each node of the linked list exactly once to perform some operation.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Untitled-Diagram-183.png\" alt=\"Data Structures\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Untitled-Diagram-183.png\">Bluepokeboy</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
